findings.remediation:
Restrict the users REST endpoint. In functions.php:
  add_filter('rest_endpoints', function($e){ unset($e['/wp/v2/users']); unset($e['/wp/v2/users/(?P<id>[\d]+)']); return $e; });
Or use a security plugin's REST hardening rule.

If you don't need this endpoint publicly, restrict it via the rest_endpoints filter:
  add_filter('rest_endpoints', function($e) {
      unset($e['/wp/v2/pages']);
      return $e;
  });

If you don't need this endpoint publicly, restrict it via the rest_endpoints filter:
  add_filter('rest_endpoints', function($e) {
      unset($e['/wp/v2/categories']);
      return $e;
  });

If you don't need this endpoint publicly, restrict it via the rest_endpoints filter:
  add_filter('rest_endpoints', function($e) {
      unset($e['/wp/v2/media']);
      return $e;
  });

If you don't need this endpoint publicly, restrict it via the rest_endpoints filter:
  add_filter('rest_endpoints', function($e) {
      unset($e['/wp/v2/types']);
      return $e;
  });

If you don't need this endpoint publicly, restrict it via the rest_endpoints filter:
  add_filter('rest_endpoints', function($e) {
      unset($e['/wp/v2/statuses']);
      return $e;
  });

If you don't integrate any external services with WordPress, disable Application Passwords:
  add_filter('wp_is_application_passwords_available', '__return_false');
If you do use them, audit /wp-admin/profile.php under each admin's profile for unrecognized entries.

Prefer AEAD ciphers (AES-GCM, CHACHA20-POLY1305). Nginx example:
  ssl_ciphers ECDHE-ECDSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256:ECDHE-RSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256:ECDHE-ECDSA-CHACHA20-POLY1305:ECDHE-RSA-CHACHA20-POLY1305:ECDHE-ECDSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384:ECDHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384;
  ssl_prefer_server_ciphers on;

If you don't use Jetpack / WP mobile / pingbacks, disable XML-RPC entirely:
  add_filter('xmlrpc_enabled', '__return_false');
Or block /xmlrpc.php at the web server.

Disable at minimum system.multicall:
  add_filter('xmlrpc_methods', function($m){ unset($m['system.multicall']); return $m; });
Or fully block /xmlrpc.php at the server.
